1. First undefined operation (EvoCC dialect, recorded command)
  Line 131 is "k = k0 * k1 * k3 * k4 * k5 * k6 * k7;". The products
  associate to the left. Lines 42 to 125 give these lane values:
    lane 0: k0 160, k1 1500, k3 2, k4 158, k5 156, k6 -150, k7 -151
    lane 1: k0 113, k1 1300, k3 4, k4 109, k5 105, k6 -100, k7 -101
  The first three products fit int: 240000, 480000 and 75840000 in lane 0,
  146900, 587600 and 64048400 in lane 1. The fourth product, by k5, does not:
    lane 0: 75840000 * 156 = 11831040000
    lane 1: 64048400 * 105 = 6725082000
  INT_MAX is 2147483647. Line 133 expects 1456467968 and -1579586240. These
  are the exact products 267973056000000 and 67923328200000 reduced modulo
  2^32. The expected output therefore depends on wrap-around.
  Every earlier operation is in range. The largest earlier value is the
  line 127 sum, 1675. The automatic record big (line 39) is never written.
  Each call to verify passes it by value. In EvoIR 3.8, a record copy moves
  uninitialized bytes and reports nothing, and both interpreters pass these
  calls. A strict reading of C11 6.3.2.1p2 can make these copies undefined
  too. That reading adds undefined behavior before line 131. It does not make
  line 131 defined.

2. Clause
  C11 6.5p5: "If an exceptional condition occurs during the evaluation of an
  expression (that is, if the result is not mathematically defined or not in
  the range of representable values for its type), the behavior is
  undefined."
  EvoIR Revision 2.1.0, Section 10.1.14, "Integer-vector profile": "other
  integer obligations and wrapping options apply per lane". The obligation is
  E3004 signed-integer-overflow. The wrapping option is -fwrapv. The recorded
  command does not give it.

3. Independent instrumented reference report on the unchanged original
  gcc-14 -std=gnu11 -O0 -Wno-psabi UB: exit 1,
    pr23135.c:131:25: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 156 * 75840000 cannot be represented in type 'int'
  gcc-14 -std=gnu11 -O2 -Wno-psabi UB: exit 1,
    pr23135.c:131:25: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 75840000 * 156 cannot be represented in type 'int'
  gcc-14 ORIG UB: exit 1, the same report as at -O2.
  Column 25 is the "*" before k5. The operands are those of point 1. The
  program stops at its first report, so GCC does not report lane 1.

4. Reason for every silent reference
  clang-19 -std=gnu11 -O0|-O2 -Wno-psabi UB and clang-19 ORIG UB: exit 0,
  no output. Silence is not evidence of definedness. The reason is Clang's
  code generator:
  - clang/lib/CodeGen/CGExprScalar.cpp at llvmorg-19.1.7, SHA256
    54652814fbec21edc0e065cf1a090720b53168542619866c11007bace676718a:
    EmitMul (line 739) adds nsw or the signed-integer-overflow check only
    when Ops.Ty->isSignedIntegerOrEnumerationType() holds (line 740). Other
    types reach the plain Builder.CreateMul (line 786).
  - clang/lib/AST/Type.cpp at llvmorg-19.1.7, SHA256
    dca95ccd790cc475752f949d45319a8703ce2d905ec1f5af724dfdf7e022e3f0:
    isSignedIntegerOrEnumerationType (lines 2167-2184) holds only for
    builtin, enumeration and _BitInt types. A vector type gives false.
  - Measured IR: vec.ll has 7 "mul <2 x i32>" (line 47 and the six products
    of line 131), 0 "mul nsw <2 x i32>", 0 llvm.smul.with.overflow calls and
    0 __ubsan_handle_mul_overflow calls. The scalar control scalar.ll has 1
    llvm.smul.with.overflow call and 1 __ubsan_handle_mul_overflow call.
  - Positive control: the scalar reduction with -DPROBE_OVERFLOW exits 1 at
    -O0 and -O2 with
    llvm-vector-overflow-reduction.c:12:17: runtime error: signed integer overflow: 75840000 * 156 cannot be represented in type 'int'
    Clang's UBSan runtime works in this image. It has no check for the
    vector product.

5. Both EvoCC interpreters
  For each F without -fwrapv, --interpret-c-hir and --interpret-evoir exit 1
  with:
    error[E3004]: signed-integer-overflow
      --> pr23135.c:131:7
       |
   131 |   k = k0 * k1 * k3 * k4 * k5 * k6 * k7;
       |       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ signed-integer-overflow
  The EvoIR interpreter adds "= note: this operation failed on the executed
  path". The span is columns 7 to 28, "k0 * k1 * k3 * k4 * k5". It is the
  product by k5, the operation of point 3. Both interpreters and GCC report
  the same kind, signed integer overflow.
  Native code traps:
    Linux, gcc-14 links the EvoCC assembly in the container: exit 133
      ("Trace/breakpoint trap") for each of the three F. A ptrace probe
      (fork, PTRACE_TRACEME, exec, continue, then read the pc and the
      instruction word at the first stop) runs the same assembly linked
      with -no-pie. For each F it stops at brk #0xbbc (word 0xd4217780),
      which is brk #3004, the signed-integer-overflow trap.
    Darwin: exit 133 (SIGTRAP) for each of the three F.
  Assembly SHA256: -O0
    63152c2ebe0f57e846d0fa96ed481dd8c8584eb064697097724557e2b7fd32ae;
    -O2 and the lane's options (identical)
    14eaa4a3d60b0387c6871a768b1c2816350c7889dac7d0065bddb341fe60549f.
  Each has 14 smull overflow checks (7 vector products, 2 lanes) and one
  brk #3004 block.

